# nettle -- the low-level crypto library GnuTLS computes with.
#
# GnuTLS does not implement ciphers itself; nettle is where AES, ChaCha20,
# SHA-2 and the elliptic curves actually live, and hogweed (built from the same
# tarball) is the public-key half that needs GMP. It is GnuTLS's ONE required
# dependency in BLFS, and unlike most "required" claims this one is structural:
# there is no alternative backend to select.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
# BLFS 12.4 pins 3.10.2 -- note the .2, not the 3.10.1 that a stale reading of
# the release list gives.
SRC_URL=https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/nettle/nettle-3.10.2.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=fe9ff51cb1f2abb5e65a6b8c10a92da0ab5ab6eaf26e7fc2b675c45f1fb519b5
SRC_DIR=nettle-3.10.2

# ==============================================================================
# --libdir=/usr/lib IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS COMMENT IS LONG
# ==============================================================================
# BLFS configures nettle as `./configure --prefix=/usr --disable-static`, with
# no --libdir. THAT COMMAND INSTALLS INTO /usr/lib64 IN THIS DISTRIBUTION'S
# BUILD IMAGES, on both architectures.
#
# nettle's configure.ac rewrites libdir itself (the block at configure.ac:406),
# and its Linux branch is:
#
#     test -d /usr/lib${ABI} \
#       && (cd /usr/lib${ABI} && pwd -P | grep >/dev/null "/lib${ABI}"'$') \
#       && libdir='${exec_prefix}/'"lib${ABI}"
#
# ABI is 64 here, so this asks: does /usr/lib64 exist, and does it resolve to a
# real path still ending in /lib64 rather than to a symlink pointing elsewhere?
# Measured in both builder images rather than assumed:
#
#     ghcr.io/duct-linux/builder:aarch64   /usr/lib64 EXISTS -> /usr/lib64
#     ghcr.io/duct-linux/builder:x86_64    /usr/lib64 EXISTS -> /usr/lib64
#
# Both satisfy it, so the book's own command line, followed exactly, puts
# libnettle.so.8 and libhogweed.so.6 in /usr/lib64 here. Verified by building
# without the flag: "configure: Libraries to be installed in
# ${exec_prefix}/lib64", and the payload lands there.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT: it is NOT a runtime failure, and an earlier draft of this
# comment claimed it was. Checked rather than assumed, in both directions:
# /usr/lib64 is line 4 of /etc/ld.so.conf -- in the builder image AND in the
# ld.so.conf that duct-filesystem ships to installed systems -- and ldconfig
# reports "/usr/lib64: (from /etc/ld.so.conf:4)". duct-filesystem creates the
# directory deliberately and gcc genuinely installs into it (libasan and the
# other sanitizer runtimes live there). So a nettle in /usr/lib64 would be
# found, would link, and would work.
#
# WHAT IT IS: a library directory chosen by PROBING THE BUILD MACHINE'S
# FILESYSTEM rather than by this distribution's policy. Every other library in
# this tree is in /usr/lib; nettle would be in /usr/lib64 for no reason anyone
# decided, and the answer would silently change if the builder image ever
# stopped shipping /usr/lib64 or started shipping it as a symlink -- the same
# recipe producing a different layout on a different day. nettle's own source
# comment concedes the guess: "Linux conventions are a mess".
#
# So this flag buys determinism and consistency, not a bug fix. That is reason
# enough to pass it, and not reason to go hunting the rest of the tree.
#
# Passing --libdir explicitly does not merely override the result, IT SKIPS THE
# GUESS ENTIRELY: the whole block is guarded by
# `if test "$libdir" = '${exec_prefix}/lib'`, which is false once --libdir is
# given. install.sh then asserts no /usr/lib64 exists in the payload, because
# the flag is the input and the directory is the outcome.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE REST
# ==============================================================================
# --disable-static. Static libraries are built by default here (`--disable-static
# Do not build any static library`), and nothing in finish_install prunes .a
# files, so omitting this ships libnettle.a and libhogweed.a.
#
# --disable-documentation. The manual is texinfo and needs makeinfo; texinfo IS
# packaged, so this is not a missing-tool workaround. It is disabled because the
# only thing it installs is an info manual for a library nothing on this system
# is developed against, and the info directory is pruned by the generic install
# anyway.
#
# NOT PASSED: --disable-public-key. It would drop hogweed entirely, and hogweed
# is exactly what GnuTLS needs for RSA, ECDSA and the key exchange in TLS --
# certificate authentication would go with it.
#
# NOT PASSED: --disable-assembler or --disable-fat. Fat builds compile several
# assembly variants and select at runtime by CPU feature, which is what makes
# one published package correct on every machine of an architecture. Disabling
# it would trade that for a small build-time saving.
#
# NOT PASSED: --enable-arm64-crypto, --enable-x86-aesni and friends. Each
# defaults to no and each compiles instructions the BUILD machine may have and a
# TARGET machine may not; the fat build already selects those paths at runtime
# after checking. Named because "enable the crypto extensions" reads like free
# performance and is actually how a package stops working on older hardware.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--libdir=/usr/lib --disable-static --disable-documentation"
